window.__labLoaded({"id":"0.3","title":"Population vs Sample","goal":"See that a sample statistic wanders around the fixed population parameter, that bigger samples wander less, and that a biased sample does not.","question":"A university has 40,000 students and wants their average weekly study time, but it can only ask a few. Before trusting any sample, we must see how a sample mean behaves next to the true population mean. We start with a population small enough to see (ten shops' sales) and then use 10,000 simulated study times.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"The lesson's ten shops","text":"Here the population is small enough to see: monthly sales of 10 shops. The population mean (a parameter) is fixed. Each sample of 3 shops gives a different sample mean (a statistic). The population SD divides by N (ddof=0), the sample SD by n - 1 (ddof=1).","code":"import numpy as np\n\nshops = np.array([12, 15, 9, 22, 18, 7, 25, 14, 20, 11])\nmu = shops.mean()\nprint(f'population mean (mu) = {mu:.2f}, sigma = {shops.std(ddof=0):.3f}')\n\nsamples = {'A': shops[[0, 3, 5]], 'B': shops[[6, 4, 1]], 'C': shops[[2, 9, 7]]}\nfor name, s in samples.items():\n    print(f'sample {name}: {s}  x-bar = {s.mean():.2f}  gap = {s.mean() - mu:+.2f}')\nprint(f'sample A sd = {samples[\"A\"].std(ddof=1):.3f}')","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for lists of numbers, as np.","Store the whole population: the monthly sales (in thousands) of all ten shops, so the truth is known exactly.","mu is the population mean. It is a parameter: one fixed number that never changes.","Print mu and sigma. ddof=0 divides by N, which is right because this is the whole population and not a sample.","Three samples of 3 shops each, picked by position. Python counts positions from 0.","Loop over the samples, taking the name and the values of each.","Show each sample's mean (a statistic) and its gap from mu. That gap is the sampling error.","Sample standard deviation with ddof=1: dividing by n - 1 makes up for a sample under-estimating the spread of the population."],"meaning":"The population mean mu is 15.30 and it never moves. The three samples give 13.67, 19.33 and 11.33, which are gaps of -1.63, +4.03 and -3.97. Each sample is honest, yet none hits 15.30. That miss is sampling error and it is normal. With only three shops, sample A's SD (7.638) can also be far from sigma (5.551).","out":"population mean (mu) = 15.30, sigma = 5.551\nsample A: [12 22  7]  x-bar = 13.67  gap = -1.63\nsample B: [25 18 15]  x-bar = 19.33  gap = +4.03\nsample C: [ 9 11 14]  x-bar = 11.33  gap = -3.97\nsample A sd = 7.638","figs":[]},{"title":"A bigger, seeded population","text":"Now a population of 10,000 weekly study times in hours, made with a fixed seed so everyone gets the same numbers. We pretend not to know its mean and compute it once: this is the parameter.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\npopulation = rng.normal(loc=14, scale=4, size=10_000).round(1)\n\nmu = population.mean()\nprint('population size N =', len(population))\nprint(f'population mean mu = {mu:.3f}')","explain":["Create a random number generator with seed 42. A fixed seed gives everyone the same 'random' numbers.","Make 10,000 weekly study times from a bell curve with mean 14 and SD 4, rounded to 0.1 hour. This is our pretend population.","Compute the population mean once. In real life it is unknown, here it is the target our samples aim at.","Print the population size N.","Print the population mean, our parameter."],"meaning":"The population holds 10,000 study times with a true mean of 13.959 hours. That is close to the 14 we aimed for, but not exactly 14, because even the population is one random draw. This fixed number is the parameter that every sample will try to hit.","out":"population size N = 10000\npopulation mean mu = 13.959","figs":[]},{"title":"Sampling error shrinks as n grows","text":"For each sample size we draw one random sample and show its gap from mu. Then we repeat the draw 300 times to see the typical gap. Bigger samples miss by less. The gap never vanishes completely: that is sampling error, and it is normal.","code":"for n in [10, 50, 250, 1000]:\n    one = rng.choice(population, size=n, replace=False).mean()\n    many = [rng.choice(population, size=n, replace=False).mean() for _ in range(300)]\n    typical = np.mean(np.abs(np.array(many) - mu))\n    print(f'n = {n:4d}: one sample mean = {one:6.2f}, typical gap over 300 samples = {typical:.3f}')","explain":["Try four sample sizes, from tiny to large.","Draw one random sample without replacement (nobody asked twice) and take its mean.","Repeat the draw 300 times to see the pattern of sample means, not just one lucky or unlucky sample.","Average the absolute gaps between those 300 sample means and mu. That is the typical sampling error.","Print the size, the one sample's mean and the typical gap."],"meaning":"The single sample means (12.40, 14.58, 14.04, 13.80) wander around 13.959. The typical gap shrinks as n grows: 1.066 at n = 10, 0.473 at 50, 0.203 at 250 and 0.098 at 1000. Using 25 times as many students cuts the error about five-fold. Bigger samples miss by less, but never by exactly zero.","out":"n =   10: one sample mean =  12.40, typical gap over 300 samples = 1.066\nn =   50: one sample mean =  14.58, typical gap over 300 samples = 0.473\nn =  250: one sample mean =  14.04, typical gap over 300 samples = 0.203\nn = 1000: one sample mean =  13.80, typical gap over 300 samples = 0.098","figs":[]},{"title":"A biased sample does not get better","text":"Suppose we only ask the 1,000 heaviest studiers (say, people we meet in the library). Even with a large sample the mean stays far from mu, because the method is tilted. A bigger sample cannot fix bias.","code":"top = np.sort(population)[-1000:]\n\nfor n in [10, 100, 1000]:\n    m = rng.choice(top, size=n, replace=False).mean()\n    print(f'biased sample n = {n:4d}: mean = {m:.2f}, gap from mu = {m - mu:+.2f}')","explain":["Sort the study times and keep the 1,000 highest: only the heaviest studiers, like asking only people in the library.","Try three sample sizes, again from small to large.","Draw a sample from that tilted group only, and take its mean.","Print the mean and its gap from the true mu, with a plus or minus sign."],"meaning":"All three means are around 21 hours, so the gaps from mu are +7.09, +6.83 and +7.09, whatever the sample size. A bigger sample shrinks random error but not bias, because the bias comes from who we ask. Choose fairly first, then make the sample large.","out":"biased sample n =   10: mean = 21.05, gap from mu = +7.09\nbiased sample n =  100: mean = 20.79, gap from mu = +6.83\nbiased sample n = 1000: mean = 21.05, gap from mu = +7.09","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"shops <- c(12, 15, 9, 22, 18, 7, 25, 14, 20, 11)\nmean(shops)\nsqrt(mean((shops - mean(shops))^2))\nA <- shops[c(1, 4, 6)]\nmean(A)\nsd(A)\nmean(shops[c(7, 5, 2)])\nmean(shops[c(3, 10, 8)])","explain":["Store the population: the sales of all ten shops, in a vector.","mean(shops) is the population mean mu.","R's sd() divides by n - 1, so for sigma we average the squared gaps ourselves (dividing by N) and take the square root.","Sample A: shops number 1, 4 and 6. R counts positions from 1.","The mean of sample A is a statistic.","sd() of sample A is the sample standard deviation (n - 1), right for a sample.","Sample B: shops 7, 5 and 2, and its mean.","Sample C: shops 3, 10 and 8, and its mean."],"expect":"mu 15.3; population sigma 5.551; sample A mean 13.67, sd 7.638; sample B mean 19.33; sample C mean 11.33","read":"R prints mu = 15.3 and sigma = 5.551, then sample A's mean 13.67 and SD 7.638, then 19.33 for sample B and 11.33 for sample C. The three statistics differ from each other and from 15.3, while mu stays put.","note":"R, SPSS and Stata have no population SD command, so R uses the formula directly. Random draws would differ from Python's because each program has its own random numbers, so we use the lesson's fixed samples."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / sales inA.\nBEGIN DATA\n12 1 15 0 9 0 22 1 18 0\n7 1 25 0 14 0 20 0 11 0\nEND DATA.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=sales.\nTEMPORARY.\nSELECT IF (inA = 1).\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=sales.","explain":["Two variables: sales, and inA, which is 1 for shops in sample A and 0 for the others.","BEGIN DATA starts the data lines.","The first five shops, each written as a sales value and then its 1 or 0 marker.","The last five shops in the same pattern.","END DATA ends the data lines.","Descriptives of sales for all ten shops: the population summary.","TEMPORARY makes the next selection last for one command only, so the full data come back afterwards.","Keep only the shops with inA = 1, which is sample A.","Descriptives again, now for sample A alone."],"read":"The first Descriptives table (N = 10) shows a Mean of 15.30, which is mu. Its Std. Deviation 5.851 is the sample version, not sigma 5.551. The second table (N = 3) shows Mean 13.67 and Std. Deviation 7.638: sample A's statistics.","note":"The first table shows mu = 15.3 for all 10 shops. The second, for sample A only, shows x-bar = 13.67 and SD 7.638. SPSS always gives the sample SD (n - 1), so for all 10 shops it shows 5.851, not the population 5.551."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 10\ngenerate sales = real(word(\"12 15 9 22 18 7 25 14 20 11\", _n))\ngenerate inA = inlist(_n, 1, 4, 6)\nsummarize sales\nsummarize sales if inA == 1","explain":["Clear any old data from memory.","Create ten empty rows, one per shop.","Fill sales from the text: word() picks the n-th number for row n and real() makes it numeric.","inA is 1 for rows 1, 4 and 6 (sample A) and 0 elsewhere. _n is the row number.","Summarize all ten shops, the population.","Summarize only the rows where inA equals 1, which is sample A."],"read":"The first summarize shows 10 observations and a Mean of 15.3 (mu). Its Std. Dev. 5.851 uses n - 1. The second shows Obs 3, Mean 13.67 and Std. Dev. 7.638, the statistics of sample A, which differ from mu.","note":"The first summarize gives mu = 15.3 for all shops, the second gives x-bar = 13.67 and SD 7.638 for sample A. Stata's SD is always the sample version, so for all 10 shops it shows 5.851."},"conclusion":"A sample statistic is not the population parameter: it wanders around it by sampling error, as our three shop samples (13.67, 19.33, 11.33 against mu = 15.30) showed. Bigger samples wander less (typical gap 1.07 at n = 10 and 0.10 at n = 1000), but a biased way of choosing, such as asking only the heaviest studiers, stays about 7 hours off at any size.","practice":{"scenario":"A landlord owns eight flats and knows the monthly electricity bill (in dollars) of every one. He wonders how well the mean of a small sample of flats would estimate the true mean of all eight, and whether asking more flats helps.","data":"bills = [52, 61, 48, 75, 66, 58, 90, 54]","tasks":["Find the population mean and the population standard deviation (ddof=0).","List every possible sample of 2 flats and of 5 flats (itertools.combinations) and compute each sample mean.","Compare the typical gap from mu for the two sample sizes, and the average of all sample means with mu."],"hint":"itertools.combinations(bills, n) gives every way to pick n flats. Take np.mean of each, then compare np.abs(means - mu).mean().","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom itertools import combinations\n\nbills = np.array([52, 61, 48, 75, 66, 58, 90, 54])\nmu = bills.mean()\nprint(f'mu = {mu:.2f}, sigma = {bills.std(ddof=0):.2f}')\nfor n in [2, 5]:\n    means = np.array([np.mean(c) for c in combinations(bills, n)])\n    print(f'n = {n}: {len(means)} samples, average of sample means = {means.mean():.2f}, '\n          f'typical gap = {np.abs(means - mu).mean():.2f}, biggest gap = {np.abs(means - mu).max():.2f}')","answer":"mu = 63.00 and sigma = 12.93. Samples of 2 give 28 possible means with a typical gap of 7.11 (the worst is 19.50). Samples of 5 give 56 means with a typical gap of 3.17 (the worst is 8.40). All sample means average exactly 63.00, so sampling is not biased, and bigger samples land closer to mu.","out":"mu = 63.00, sigma = 12.93\nn = 2: 28 samples, average of sample means = 63.00, typical gap = 7.11, biggest gap = 19.50\nn = 5: 56 samples, average of sample means = 63.00, typical gap = 3.17, biggest gap = 8.40","figs":[]}}});
